* Popular Deep Learning Models
** Core Characteristics
*** Definition: Neural networks with multiple layers; learn hierarchical representations via backpropagation.
*** Strengths: High performance on complex data (images, text, sequences); scalable with data/GPUs.
*** Weaknesses: Data-hungry; computationally intensive; less interpretable; risk of overfitting.
** Feedforward Neural Networks (MLP)
*** Tasks: Tabular data regression/classification; baseline for structured data.
*** Why Chosen: Simple fully connected layers; universal function approximators.
*** Details: Dense layers with activations (ReLU); used when spatial/temporal structure is absent.
*** Variants: Wide & Deep models; often outperformed by specialized architectures.
** Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)
*** Tasks: Image classification, object detection, segmentation; time series/1D signals.
*** Why Chosen: Exploit spatial locality via convolutions; parameter sharing; translation invariance.
*** Key Components: Conv layers, pooling, batch norm; heads for tasks.
*** Classic/Early
**** LeNet (1998): Digit recognition; small conv-pool stacks.
**** AlexNet (2012): Deep ReLU + Dropout; ImageNet winner.
**** VGG (2014): Uniform 3x3 convs; strong feature extractor.
*** Depth-Optimized
**** ResNet (2015): Residual connections; enables 100+ layers; mitigates vanishing gradients.
**** Variants: ResNet-50/101/152; widely used backbone.
*** Efficiency-Focused
**** MobileNet (V1-V3): Depthwise separable convs; lightweight for mobile/edge.
**** EfficientNet (B0-B7): Compound scaling of depth/width/resolution; SOTA accuracy-efficiency.
*** Multi-Scale/Parallel
**** Inception/GoogLeNet: Parallel convs (1x1,3x3,5x5); reduces params.
**** Xception: Extreme Inception with depthwise separables.
*** Dense Connectivity
**** DenseNet: Each layer connects to all subsequent; feature reuse; fewer params.
*** Modern CNNs
**** ConvNeXt (2022): Modernized ResNet; competes with Transformers.
** Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs)
*** Tasks: Sequence modeling (early); largely superseded by Transformers.
*** Why Chosen: Handle variable-length sequences; maintain hidden state.
*** Details: Backpropagation through time; vanishing/exploding gradients.
*** Variants
**** LSTM: Gated memory cells; long-term dependencies.
**** GRU: Simpler gating; similar performance to LSTM.
** Transformers
*** Tasks: NLP (translation, generation); vision (ViT); multimodal; now dominant.
*** Why Chosen: Self-attention enables parallelization; captures long-range dependencies; scalable.
*** Details: Encoder-decoder; positional encodings; multi-head attention.
*** NLP-Focused
**** BERT (2018): Bidirectional encoder; pretraining on masked LM; fine-tune for tasks.
**** GPT Series (2018+): Decoder-only; autoregressive generation; scaling laws.
**** T5: Text-to-text framework; unified pretraining.
*** Vision Transformers (ViT)
**** Tasks: Image classification; patch-based tokens.
**** Why Chosen: Outperforms CNNs on large data; no inductive bias but highly scalable.
*** Multimodal/Large Models
**** CLIP: Contrastive image-text pretraining; zero-shot capabilities.
**** Flamingo/DALL-E: Vision-language models; image generation from text.
** Generative Models
*** Autoencoders
**** Tasks: Denoising, anomaly detection, feature learning.
**** Variants: Variational Autoencoders (VAE) - Probabilistic latent space; generative sampling.
*** Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)
**** Tasks: Image/video synthesis, style transfer.
**** Why Chosen: Produces high-quality samples via min-max game.
**** Details: Generator vs Discriminator; training instability.
**** Variants: DCGAN (convolutional), StyleGAN (progressive/style-based), BigGAN (large-scale).
*** Diffusion Models (2020+)
**** Tasks: State-of-the-art image/video generation (e.g., Stable Diffusion, Sora).
**** Why Chosen: Stable training; superior sample quality/diversity.
**** Details: Forward noise addition + reverse denoising; U-Net backbone.
** Graph Neural Networks (GNNs)
*** Tasks: Node/edge/graph prediction on structured graphs (social nets, molecules).
*** Why Chosen: Handle non-Euclidean data; message passing paradigm.
*** Details: Aggregates neighbor information.
*** Variants: GCN (convolutional), GAT (attention), GraphSAGE (sampling).
** Key Considerations
*** Pretraining & Transfer Learning: Crucial (ImageNet, BERT, CLIP); fine-tune to save resources.
*** Architectures Trends (2025): Scaling (larger models), efficiency (quantization/pruning), multimodal integration.
*** Task-Specific: Choose CNNs for grids, Transformers for sequences/text, hybrids for multimodal.
*** Evaluation: Task metrics + efficiency (FLOPs, latency); benchmarks like GLUE, ImageNet, COCO.
*** Frameworks: PyTorch/TensorFlow/JAX; libraries like Hugging Face Transformers.
